Phase 1:

In this phase, I created an abstract VehicleReport class that uses a template method to run data collection,
report formatting, and status logging in order. The Hollywood Principle means the base class controls the process and
calls the subclass's formatting method when needed, instead of the subclass deciding when to call each method.
Making generateReport() final ensures that subclasses cannot change the order of the steps.

Phase 2: 

When I called generateReport() on both objects, I noticed that the VehicleReport base class controlled the execution
order while each subclass provided its own formatting. The most intuitive part was reusing the same workflow,
while the tricky part was understanding how an abstract class can define the process without implementing every method itself.
